{"path": "report/main.tex", "content": "\documentclass[sloppy]{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{url}

\title{Transcriptional Metabolic States in Meningioma Brain Single-Cell Data}
\author{}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This study investigates whether transcriptional metabolic states can be identified in single-cell RNA-seq data from meningioma brain samples using Reactome metabolic pathway genes. We analyze 58,843 cells from 10 samples using 1,984 metabolic genes from Reactome pathway R-HSA-1430728.
\end{abstract}

\section{Data and Methods}

\subsection{Dataset}
The Choudhury et al. 2022 study (3CA ID: 3ca:20773) provides single-cell RNA-seq data from 10 meningioma brain samples with 58,843 cells and 33,538 detected genes. The analysis uses 1,984 metabolic genes from Reactome pathway R-HSA-1430728 (release 97).

\subsection{Normalization and Feature Selection}
Expression data was normalized using per-cell library size scaling to 10,000 counts, followed by log transformation:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:normalization}
\log_{10}(x + 1) = \log_{10}(1 + x)
\end{equation}
where $x$ represents the normalized expression value.

\subsection{Clustering Analysis}
We performed Leiden clustering on the metabolic gene subset with multiple seeds and resolutions. The analysis also included HVG-based and random control comparisons to establish baselines.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Global Clustering Analysis}
Leiden clustering with seeds [0, 17, 42, 73, 101] at resolution 0.4 identified 15 clusters. The clustering metrics for 3,000 metric cells are:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\toprule
Metric & Value \\
\midrule
Silhouette & 0.229 \\
Calinski-Harabasz & 7,177.0 \\
Davies-Bouldin & 1.513 \\
Mean Pairwise ARI & 0.980 \\
Min Pairwise ARI & 0.973 \\
Median Silhouette & 0.221 \\
\bottomrule
\end{table}

\subsection{Random Control Comparison}
We compared the metabolic clustering against 19 random control clusters of the same size. Zero random controls exceeded or equaled the metabolic clustering in silhouette score. The add-one rank fraction is 0.05 (descriptive, not an inferential p-value).

\subsection{Cell Type Associations}
Confounding factors were checked including patient, sample, source, cell_type, cell_subtype, cell_cycle_phase, complexity, computed_total_counts, computed_detected_genes, and computed_mito_percent. The patient within-replicate ARI ranged from 0.106 to 0.813, with the weakest replicate being sample 3 (3,287 cells). The patient NMI was 0.544, cell type NMI was 0.507, and cell subtype NMI was 0.658.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Can Metabolic Genes Form Computational Clusters?}
Yes, the metabolic genes can form computational clusters. Leiden clustering identified 15 distinct groupings of cells based on metabolic gene expression patterns. The clustering achieved a silhouette score of 0.229 and mean pairwise ARI of 0.980, indicating reasonable cluster stability.

\subsection{Do These Clusters Represent Distinct Biological Metabolic States?}
The analysis is inconclusive regarding whether these clusters represent distinct biological metabolic states. While candidate partitions were observed, the presence of clusters does not prove distinct biological metabolic states. The clustering could reflect technical variation, cell cycle effects, or other non-metabolic factors. The random control comparison shows that metabolic clustering is not significantly better than random, and the confounder NMI values suggest potential associations with sample and cell type.

\section{Conclusion}
Metabolic genes can computationally cluster the single-cell RNA-seq data, but this does not prove the existence of distinct biological metabolic states. Further validation with orthogonal methods and careful confounder control is needed to establish biological significance.

